      "S2"
    ],
    "word_count": 50,
    "html": "<p>Christina Onassis was a Greek shipping heiress and businesswoman, born in New York to Aristotle Onassis and Athina Livanos. She inherited a substantial share of her father's shipping fortune and became the first woman elected to the Union of Greek Shipowners' board. Her daughter, Athina, succeeded to her private estate.</p>\r\n"
  },
  "long_biography": {
    "plain_text": "Shipping heiress Christina Onassis began her business education in the family's Manhattan office, working as a secretary at eighteen. Her father took her to meetings, while specialists taught her about the group's international affairs. Fluent in several languages, she learned the practical demands of managing interests spread across countries.\n\nShe also served as president of the Alexander S. Onassis Public Benefit Foundation, established in memory of her brother. During its early years, the foundation began awarding scholarships. She helped bring a new generation into its leadership, recommending lawyer and economist Anthony Papadimitriou for its board in 1988.",
    "confidence": {
      "score": 93,
      "band": "high",
      "reason": "Contemporary account supports practical training and languages; primary foundation history and Papadimitriou's first-person interview support presidency and recruitment. Later institutional achievements are not attributed to her."
    },
    "source_ids": [
      "S1",
      "S4",
      "S5"
    ],
    "word_count": 97,
    "html": "<p>Shipping heiress Christina Onassis began her business education in the family's Manhattan office, working as a secretary at eighteen. Her father took her to meetings, while specialists taught her about the group's international affairs. Fluent in several languages, she learned the practical demands of managing interests spread across countries.</p>\r\n<p>She also served as president of the Alexander S. Onassis Public Benefit Foundation, established in memory of her brother. During its early years, the foundation began awarding scholarships. She helped bring a new generation into its leadership, recommending lawyer and economist Anthony Papadimitriou for its board in 1988.</p>\r\n"
  },
